{"name":"Caviar Couture","hex":"#772244","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"caviar","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Roe of the sturgeon or of certain other large fish, considered a delicacy.\n A food preparation made through the spherification of a flavorful liquid, resulting in small roe-like balls similar in texture and appearance to caviar.","A food preparation made through the spherification of a flavorful liquid, resulting in small roe-like balls similar in texture and appearance to caviar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caviar"},{"term":"couture","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The production of high-end, custom-made clothing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/couture"}],"entries":[{"title":"Caviar","blocks":[{"text":"Caviar or caviare is a food consisting of salt-cured roe of the family Acipenseridae (sturgeon). Caviar is considered a delicacy and is eaten as a garnish or spread. Traditionally, the term caviar refers only to roe from wild sturgeon in the Caspian Sea and Black Sea. The term caviar can also describe the roe of other species of sturgeon or other fish such as paddlefish, salmon, steelhead, trout, lumpfish, whitefish, or carp."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Caviar_spoons.jpg/330px-Caviar_spoons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caviar","wikidata":"Q186385"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Beluga_sturgeon.png/500px-Beluga_sturgeon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Beluga_sturgeon.png/500px-Beluga_sturgeon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The rarest and most expensive form of caviar comes from the critically endangered beluga sturgeon that swims in the